WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.917
Cardinality describes how many rows can match across a relationship.

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:09.457
In a one-to-one relationship, each key matches at most one row on each side.

00:00:09.457 --> 00:00:15.167
In a one-to-many relationship, one parent row can match several child rows.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:21.707
A patient-to-admissions relationship is one-to-many because one patient can have several admissions.

00:00:21.707 --> 00:00:28.257
The larger risk appears when several one-to-many child tables are joined independently to the same parent.

00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:34.407
Suppose one patient has two admissions, two lab results, and two prescriptions.

00:00:34.407 --> 00:00:38.732
If we join all three detail tables directly by patient ID,

00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:44.072
the database can produce two times two times two, or eight, combinations.

00:00:44.072 --> 00:00:47.837
Those eight rows do not represent eight new medical events.

00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:50.927
They represent combinations of existing events.

00:00:50.927 --> 00:00:54.977
This is a common source of inflated counts in analytical SQL.

00:00:54.977 --> 00:01:00.897
Before joining, estimate whether each relationship is zero-to-one, one-to-one, or one-to-many.

00:01:00.897 --> 00:01:06.972
That prediction helps you recognize when the output row count is reasonable and when multiplication is

00:01:06.972 --> 00:01:09.003
occurring.
